  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1044 PM MST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nThere has been almost no precipitation in the high country this \nafternoon and evening. Additionally, there is practically no chance \nfor precipitation through the rest of the night and beyond for our \nforecast area. Lows tonight are forecast to be similar to the last \nfew nights with temperatures around freezing for the plains and in \nthe upper teens to 20s for the high country.\n\nAt the risk of sounding like a broken record... the next 5 days and \nlikely beyond look warm with above average temperatures, abundant \nsunshine, and near-zero chances for any precipitation. Current water \nvapor imagery shows an upper-level ridge starting to build over \nCalifornia and the Great Basin. This ridge will quickly expand to \nthe east on Saturday influencing our weather for the foreseeable \nfuture. Highs on Saturday are forecast to top out a bit warmer than \nFriday with low to mid 60s across the plains. There will be less \nclouds than Friday given increased subsidence. Sunday and Monday \nremain on track to be the warmest days of the week with the mid-\nlevel ridge peaking in strength for our area. It is even trending a \nbit stronger in recent model runs. Ensemble guidance remains in good \nagreement of high temperatures in the upper 60s to approaching 70 \nfor the urban corridor and eastern plains for Sunday and Monday. \nEven then, for Tuesday through at least Friday, ensemble guidance \nshows highs for the plains staying in the low to mid 60s. There \ncould be some slight fluctuations in high temperatures during this \nperiod depending on if we can get some cold air advection as well as \nless of an influence from the upper-level ridge. Even then, no \nsignificant cooldown is on the horizon through at least the end of \nnext week. A trough looks to pass off to our northeast on Wednesday, \nbut could bring some breezier conditions for the eastern plains. \nThere is still a broad consensus that the ridge should break down by \nnext weekend, which generally looks to bring cooler temperatures and \ncould bring at least some chance for precipitation as we approach \nthe 3rd week of November.",
